%[text] %[text:anchor:T_BD316965] # Transverse Shear Locking in Shear-Deformable Plates `-` Cantilever Plate Subject to Vertical Distributed Load
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D547C230] ## Introduction
%[text] 
%[text] Transverse-shear locking manifests in shear-deformable elements by means of spurious transverse-shear forces that render the element's behavior artificially stiffer than it should be. This effect has the following implications in the case that transverse-shear locking is apparent:
%[text] - The shear-force distribution is oscillatory
%[text] - The deformations are artifially stiffer than they should be
%[text] - The convergence rate is considerably reduced or even halted overall
%[text] - The effect of transverse-shear locking is proportional to the slenderness of the plate: the more slender the plate is, the more pronounced the effect of locking becomes \
%[text] Selective reduced integration is not applicable in the case of two-dimensional elements. The reason is, that in two-dimensional plate elements 4 Gauss points are needed to correctly integrate the shear-part of the stiffness in the frame of the underlying `2x2` quadrature. Using one point per direction, the latter `2x2` quadrature reduces to a single Gauss point, namely, 3 Gauss points are removed. As a result, spurious energy modes become apparent and thus the global stiffness matrix becomes singular. These additional spurious energy modes need then be supressed, so that selective reduced integration can be effectively applied for two-dimensional shear-deformable plate elements. Other methods for the alleviation of transverse shear locking for two-dimensional plate elements include:
%[text] - Use of high-order basis functions
%[text] - Use of advanced techniques such as Assumed Natural Strains (ANS) or other (EAS, etc.) \
%[text] The problem addressed herein is that of a rectangular plate subject to distributed vertical load, acting as a cantilever plate, see the following figure:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![problemSetting_cantilever_plate.png](text:image:41b2)
%[text] The analytical solution of the latter setup is discussed in Live Script [`main_Chapter3_AShearDeformablePlateElement.m`](file:....\3_ReissnerMindlinPlate\main_Chapter3_AShearDeformablePlateElement.m), namely,
%[text] $w(X,Y) = \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} \\left( \\overline{p} L X - \\frac{\\overline{p} X^2}{2} \\right) - \\frac{1}{EI} \\left( - \\frac{\\overline{p} X^4}{24} + \\frac{\\overline{p} L X^3}{6} - \\frac{\\overline{p} L^2 X^2}{4} \\right) \\qquad (1.1) \\\\\n\\beta(X,Y) = \\int\_0^L - \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} \\overline{p} - \\frac{\\text{d}^2 w}{\\text{d} x^2} \\; \\text{d} X \\qquad \\qquad \\qquad \\qquad \\;\\;\\,\\qquad  \\qquad  \\quad \\;\\; (1.2)$
%[text] The analytical solution in Eqs. (1.1) and (1.2) can be obtained in a single line of code using the [Symbolic Math Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/symbolic.html)™ in [MATLAB](https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab.html)®, see this [code section](internal:H_518CB8A7).
%[text] This solution will be then used to determine the resultant transverse shear force $q\_x$, which is given in this case by the following relationship:
%[text] $q\_x(X,Y) = \\alpha G A \\gamma\_x(X,Y) = \\alpha G A \\left( \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y \\right) \\qquad (2)$
%[text] We will then use this reaction transverse shear force to demonstrate how transverse shear locking can be detected.
%[text] 
%[text] [PREVIOUS Chapter](file:..\4_TransverseShearLocking_TimoshenkoBeam\main_Chapter4_TransverseShearLocking_TimoshenkoBeam.m) *Transverse-Shear Locking in Timoshenko beam elements - Cantilever Beam Subject to Vertical Distributed Load*
%[text] [NEXT Chapter](file:..\4_TransverseShearLocking_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate\main_Chapter4_Locking_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate.m) *Transverse-Shear Locking in Shear-Deformable Plates* *`-`* *Two Sided Clampled Plate Subject to Vertical Distributed Load*
%[text] 
%[text] **Author: Andreas Apostolatos, PhD (aapostol@mathworks.com)**
%[text] **Date: 20.02.2023**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF2E5D07] ## Preamble
clc;
clear;
close all;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F78170C0] ## **Problem parameters**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B5E52518] ### Geometric parameters
%[text] Thickness of the plate \[m\]
propStr.t = 10^(-1); %[control:slider:8628]{"position":[17,19]} %[text:anchor:M_08BBF242]
%[text] X-coordinate of left corner \[m\]
X0 = 0;
%[text] X-coordinate of right corner \[m\]
XLx = 4;
%[text] Y-coordinate of left corner \[m\]
Y0 = 0;
%[text] Y-coordinate of right corner \[m\]
YLy = 1;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FD0F76EA] ### Material paramerters
%[text] Distributed load \[N/m^2\]
propStr.pBar = -1e2;
%[text] Distributed moment around x-axis \[N/m\]
propStr.mxBar = 0;
%[text] Distributed moment around y-axis \[N/m\]
propStr.myBar = 0;
%[text] Young's modulus \[N/m^2\]
propStr.E = 1e7;
%[text] Poisson ratio \[dimensionless\]
propStr.nu = 0;
%[text] Shear modulus (connected to epsilon\_12 = E/(1+nu)) \[N/m^2\]
propStr.G = propStr.E/2/(1 + propStr.nu);
%[text] Plate's stiffness \[Nm\]
propStr.D = propStr.E*propStr.t^3/12/(1 - propStr.nu^2);
%[text] Shear-correction factor
propStr.alpha = 5/6;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_890F1CC2] ### Newton-Raphson properties for the inversion of the geometry map
%[text] Convergence tolerance
propNewtonRaphson.eps = 1e-9;
%[text] Maximum number of iterations
propNewtonRaphson.maxIt = 10;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_518CB8A7] ## Analytical solution using the Timoshenko beam formulation
%[text] Since the plate is rectangular, clamped on its one end while being subject to constant distributed vertical load its behavior is expected to resemble a beam aligned with the X-axis, whereas no effects are expected to take place along the Y-axis. The analytical solution for this problem using the shear-deformable Timoshenko beam formulation are in this case as follows
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B05D0DF2] ### Auxiliary variables
syms wEx(X) betayEx(X) qxEx(X) Lsym psym Gsym Aqsym ...
    Esym Isym tsym alphaScf
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E243EEEE] ### Analytical solution with respect to the vertical deflection
%[text] Set the analytical solution in terms of the vertical deflection field (Eq. (1) in the introduction):
%[text] $w(X,Y) = \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} \\left( \\overline{p} L X - \\frac{\\overline{p} X^2}{2} \\right) - \\frac{1}{EI} \\left( - \\frac{\\overline{p} X^4}{24} + \\frac{\\overline{p} L X^3}{6} - \\frac{\\overline{p} L^2 X^2}{4} \\right) \\qquad (3)$
%[text] namely:
wEx(X) = (psym*Lsym*X-psym*X^2/2)/Gsym/(Aqsym) - ...
    (-psym*X^4/24+psym*Lsym*X^3/6-psym*Lsym^2*X^2/4)/Esym/Isym;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51569EC4] ### Analytical solution with respect to the transverse-shear forces
%[text] Get the analytical solution of the rotational field by leveraging the Partial Differential Equation (PDE) of the problem and the functions of the [Symbolic Math Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/symbolic.html) in [MATLAB](https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab.html)
%[text] $\\beta(X,y) = \\int\_{0}^L - \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A}\\overline{p} - \\frac{\\partial^2 w}{\\partial x^2} \\: \\text{d}x  \\qquad (4)$
betayEx(X) = int(-1/(propStr.alpha*propStr.G*YLy*propStr.t)*...
    propStr.pBar - diff(diff(wEx, X), X));
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4FF8DB3D] ### Analytical solution with respect to the transverse-shear forces
%[text] Herein we are concerned with the resultant transverse-shear force component $q\_x(X,Y) = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{\\left( \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y \\right)}^{\\gamma\_x}$ because $q\_y(X,Y)$ is identically zero across the plate due to the given setup. Resultant transverse-shear force component $q\_x(X,Y) $ is given by:
qxEx(X) = alphaScf*Gsym*tsym*(diff(wEx(X), X, 1) + betayEx(X));
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_42CC7FA8] ### Substitution of the known parameters in the analytical expression
wExSubs(X) = subs(wEx,{Lsym, psym, Gsym, Esym, Isym, Aqsym}, ...
    {XLx, propStr.pBar, propStr.G, propStr.E, ...
    YLy*(propStr.t^3)/12, propStr.alpha*YLy*propStr.t});
betayExSubs(X) = subs(betayEx, {Lsym, psym, Gsym, Esym, Isym, Aqsym}, ...
    {XLx, propStr.pBar, propStr.G, propStr.E, YLy*(propStr.t^3)/12, ...
    propStr.alpha*YLy*propStr.t});
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_486D47F1] ## Analytical expressions for the two-dimensional basis functions
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_25760CDC] ### Analytical expressions for the bilinear basis functions
%[text] $N\_1^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 - \\xi)(1 - \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad (5.1) \\\\\nN\_2^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 + \\xi)(1 - \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad (5.2) \\\\\nN\_3^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 + \\xi)(1 + \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad (5.3) \\\\\nN\_4^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 - \\xi)(1 + \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\]. \\quad (5.4)$
syms N1Q1(xi, eta) N2Q1(xi, eta) N3Q1(xi, eta) N4Q1(xi, eta)
N1Q1(xi, eta) = (1 - xi)*(1 - eta)/4;
N2Q1(xi, eta) = (1 + xi)*(1 - eta)/4;
N3Q1(xi, eta) = (1 + xi)*(1 + eta)/4;
N4Q1(xi, eta) = (1 - xi)*(1 + eta)/4;
NQ1 = [N1Q1 N4Q1 N3Q1 N2Q1];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CDEC875F] ### Analytical expressions for the biquadratic basis functions
%[text] $N\_1^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4} \\xi (\\xi - 1) \\eta (1 - \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (6.1)  \\\\\nN\_2^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}(\\xi + 1)(\\xi - 1)\\eta(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (6.2) \\\\\nN\_3^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(\\xi + 1)\\xi \\eta(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (6.3) \\\\\nN\_4^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}(\\xi + 1)\\xi(\\eta + 1)(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (6.4) \\\\\nN\_5^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(\\xi + 1)\\xi(\\eta + 1)\\eta \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (6.5) \\\\\nN\_6^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}(\\xi + 1)(\\xi - 1)(\\eta + 1)\\eta \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (6.6) \\\\\nN\_7^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}\\xi (\\xi - 1)(\\eta + 1)\\eta \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\],  \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (6.7) \\\\\nN\_8^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\xi (\\xi - 1)(\\eta + 1)(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (6.8) \\\\\nN\_9^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{1}(1 - \\xi^2) (1 - eta^2) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \n \\quad\\ \\quad \\quad \\quad (6.9)\\\\$
syms N1Q2(xi, eta) N2Q2(xi, eta) N3Q2(xi, eta) N4Q2(xi, eta) ...
    N5Q2(xi, eta) N6Q2(xi, eta) N7Q2(xi, eta) N8Q2(xi, eta) ...
    N9Q2(xi, eta)
N1Q2(xi, eta) = xi*(xi - 1)*eta*(eta - 1)/4;
N2Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*(xi - 1)*eta*(eta - 1)/-2;
N3Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*xi*eta*(eta - 1)/4;
N4Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*xi*(eta + 1)*(eta - 1)/-2;
N5Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*xi*(eta + 1)*eta/4;
N6Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*eta/-2;
N7Q2(xi, eta) = xi*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*eta/4;
N8Q2(xi, eta) = xi*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*(eta - 1)/-2;
N9Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*(eta - 1)/1;
NQ2 = [N1Q2 N7Q2 N5Q2 N3Q2 N8Q2 N4Q2 N2Q2 N6Q2 N9Q2];
%[text] ![](text:image:53a4) **Reflect**
%[text] How can the setup of these interpolatory basis functions be generalized?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_76F0D5D7] ## **Discretization**
%[text] Both the vertical deflection $w$ and cross-sectional rotation $\\beta\_x$, $\\beta\_y$ fields are discretized using the same basis (shape) functions, namely:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nw\\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\;w\_i ,\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(7\\ldotp 1\\right)\\\\\n\\beta\_x \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\;\\beta\_{x,i} \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(7\\ldotp 2\\right)\\\\\n\\beta\_y \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\;\\beta\_{y,i} \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(7\\ldotp 3\\right)\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Here $N$ indicates the total number of nodes in the mesh. Moreover a Buvnov-Galerkin discretization is used, meaning that both the test and the unknown fields are discretized using the same basis (shape) functions.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_98497D7F] ### **Quadrilateral mesh**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B0B09769] #### Choose number of elements in the mesh
%[text] Number of elements along the X-direction
numElx = 12;
%[text] Number of elements along the Y-direction
numEly = 3;
%[text] Total number of elements
numEl = numElx*numEly;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FA5BE8CD] #### Generation of a quadrilateral mesh
[nodesX, nodesY] = ...
    generateQuadrilateralMesh(X0, XLx, Y0, YLy, numElx, numEly);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C8B5926E] #### Rendering the mesh non-uniform
%[text] Check the following checkbox to render the mesh non-uniform **Note** This only works for numElx = 4 and numEly = 2 with the given values
isMeshNonUniform = false; %[control:checkbox:7787]{"position":[20,25]} %[text:anchor:M_8617C6FE]
if isMeshNonUniform
    if numElx ~= 4 || numEly ~= 2
        error("Making the mesh non-uniform only works for numElx = 4 and numEly = 2")
    end
%[text] Displace node 2
    nodesY(2, 1) = nodesY(2, 1) + 0.08; % y
%[text] Displace node 5
    nodesX(2, 2) = nodesX(2, 2) + 0.08; % x
    nodesY(5, 1) = nodesY(5, 1) - 0.08; % y    
%[text] Displace node 8
    nodesX(2, 3) = nodesX(2, 3) - 0.08; % x
    nodesY(8, 1) = nodesY(8, 1) + 0.08; % y
%[text] Displace node 11
    nodesX(2, 4) = nodesX(2, 4) + 0.08; % x
    nodesY(11, 1) = nodesY(11, 1) + 0.08; % x
%[text] displace node 14
    nodesY(14, 1) = nodesY(14, 1) - 0.08; % x
%[text] 
end
%[text] ![](text:image:9056) **Try**
%[text] Use a non-uniform mesh by unselecting the checkbox in this [code section](internal:M_8617C6FE).
%[text] ![](text:image:4741) **Reflect**
%[text] Do you still expect a numerical solution from FEM that matches the analytical one?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4A4EF0A6] ### **Four-noded bilinear quadrilateral mesh**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_828BAEA7] #### Mesh generation
numNodesElQ1 = 4;
mshQ1 = generateBilinearQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numElx, numEly, numEl, nodesX, nodesY, numNodesElQ1);
numNodesQ1 = height(mshQ1.nodes);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E4C0AE9E] #### Dirichlet boundary conditions for the mesh with the bilinear quadrilateral elements
[freeDOFsQ1, homDOFsQ1] = ...
    utilityFunctionsCantileverPlate.getFreeDOFsBilinearQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numNodesQ1, numEly);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0A24F150] #### Mesh plot
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshQ1, numVertices, true); %[output:85dab139]
hold on; %[output:85dab139]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshQ1.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:85dab139]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:85dab139]
end
hold off; %[output:85dab139]
axis equal; %[output:85dab139]
xlabel("$x$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:85dab139]
ylabel("$y$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:85dab139]
zlabel("$z$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:85dab139]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2B6D8341] #### Display the mesh with the basis functions
isPlotBasisFunctions = false; %[control:checkbox:4d27]{"position":[24,29]} %[text:anchor:M_40C41664]
if isPlotBasisFunctions
    syms xi eta
    NQ1Vis = {(1 - xi)*(1 - eta)/4, (1 - xi)*(1 + eta)/4, ...
        (1 + xi)*(1 + eta)/4, (1 + xi)*(1 - eta)/4};
    [h5, h6, h7] = plotMeshAndBasisFunctions2d(mshQ1, NQ1Vis, true);
end
%[text] ![](text:image:7987) **Try**
%[text] Select the checkbox in this [code section](internal:M_40C41664) to create an animation for the bilinear basis functions throughout the mesh. Be aware that this code section can take very long to run due to rendering of complex graphics.
%[text] ![](text:image:619d) **Reflect**
%[text] What is the support of the bilinear basis functions, namely, the area over which they are not identical zero?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4E93D9EB] ### **Nine-noded biquadratic quadrilateral mesh**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1AC37C56] #### Mesh generation
numNodesElQ2 = 9;
[mshQ2, ~] = generateBiquadraticQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numElx, numEly, nodesX, nodesY, mshQ1, numNodesElQ1, numNodesElQ2);
numNodesQ2 = height(mshQ2.nodes);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CC6D5D21] #### Dirichlet boundary conditions for the mesh with the biquadratic quadrilateral elements
[freeDOFsQ2, homDOFsQ2] = ...
    utilityFunctionsCantileverPlate.getFreeDOFsBiquadraticQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numNodesQ1, numNodesQ2, numEly);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A156E8F7] #### Mesh plot
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshQ2, numVertices, true); %[output:601bbc0a]
hold on; %[output:601bbc0a]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ2, ysQ2, zsQ2] = createSupports3D(mshQ2.nodes, homDOFsQ2, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ2(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ2(k, :), ysQ2(k, :), zsQ2(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:601bbc0a]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:601bbc0a]
end
hold off; %[output:601bbc0a]
axis equal; %[output:601bbc0a]
xlabel("x") %[output:601bbc0a]
ylabel("y") %[output:601bbc0a]
zlabel("z") %[output:601bbc0a]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2846EBBD] ## Master stiffness matrix and load vector
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E82E61EE] ### Four-noded bilinear Reissner-Mindlin elements
computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ1 = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ1 = ...
    @computeBilinearBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
[KQ1, FQ1] = computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate ...
    (mshQ1, computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ1, ...
    computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ1, propStr);
%[text] Sparsity of the stiffness matrix
spy(KQ1) %[output:559401fc]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C988B40A] ![](text:image:279b) **Try**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_85986FA8] Computation the master stiffness matrix corresponding to the reduced integration
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorRI = ...
%[text]     @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlateRI;
%[text] computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVector = ...
%[text]     @computeBilinearBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
%[text] [K_Q1_RI, F_Q1_RI] = ...
%[text]     computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate ...
%[text]     (msh, computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVector, ...
%[text]     computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorRI, propStr);
%[text] spy(K_RI) % visualization of the sparsity of the stiffness matrix when using selective-reduced integration for the biquadratic elements
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:138f) **Reflect**
%[text] Compute the rank of the stiffness matrix when using the selective reduced-integration. 
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] rank_K_RI = rank(K_RI);
%[text] rbm = height(K_RI) - rank_K_RI;
%[text] disp("The stiffness matrix computed using " + ...
%[text]     "the selective reduced-integration possesses " + ...
%[text]     rbm + " rigid body modes");
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CB3E1720] ### Nine-noded biquadratic Reissner-Mindlin elements
computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ2 = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ2 = ...
    @computeBiquadraticBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
[KQ2, FQ2] = computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate ...
    (mshQ2, computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ2, ...
    computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ2, propStr);
%[text] Sparsity of the master global stiffness matrix
spy(KQ2) %[output:65fefcef]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_792DA900] #### ![](text:image:1624) **Reflect**
%[text] Why is the sparsity pattern of the master stiffness matrix not aligned around the main-diagonal for the case of the biquadratic quadrilateral elements as compared to the bilinear quadrilateral elements?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_78FFE007] ## Solution of the Reissner-Mindlin plate problem
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_052C3249] ### Four-noded bilinear elements
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1CF74794] #### Solution of the linear equation system
numDOFsQ1 = 2*numNodesQ1;
uQ1 = zeros(numDOFsQ1, 1);
uQ1(freeDOFsQ1, 1) = KQ1(freeDOFsQ1, freeDOFsQ1)\FQ1(freeDOFsQ1);
%[text] ![](text:image:15d3) **Try**
%[text] Solve the linear equation system using the selective reduced integration. Is the linear equation system solvable?
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] u_Q1_RI(freeDOFs_Q1, 2) = K_Q1_RI(freeDOFs_Q1, freeDOFs_Q1)\F_Q1_RI(freeDOFs_Q1);
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:7a27) **Reflect**
%[text] Name possible methods to circumvent this issue from the literature
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B612F0E2] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the vertical displacement field
mshCurrQ1 = mshQ1;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:73a8]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ1.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ1.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ1(1:3:end, 1);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
figure, %[output:0b93dd38]
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ1, numVertices, false); %[output:0b93dd38]
hold on; %[output:0b93dd38]
fsurf(wExSubs, [X0 XLx Y0 YLy], FaceColor=[203 65 84]/255, ... %[output:0b93dd38]
    FaceAlpha=0.5, LineStyle="none"); %[output:0b93dd38]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:0b93dd38]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:0b93dd38]
end
hold off; %[output:0b93dd38]
axis equal; %[output:0b93dd38]
xlabel("$x$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:0b93dd38]
ylabel("$y$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:0b93dd38]
zlabel("$z$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:0b93dd38]
legend("FEM", "", "analytical") %[output:0b93dd38]
title("Plate's deformation using Q1 elements") %[output:0b93dd38]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_73CF2D17] ### Visualization of the solution in terms of the rotational field
mshCurrQ1Betay = mshQ1;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:77b9]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ1Betay.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ1Betay.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ1(3:3:end);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
figure, %[output:2ac873ee]
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ1Betay, numVertices, false); %[output:2ac873ee]
hold on; %[output:2ac873ee]
fsurf(betayExSubs, [X0 XLx Y0 YLy], FaceColor=[203 65 84]/255, ... %[output:2ac873ee]
    FaceAlpha=0.5, LineStyle="none"); %[output:2ac873ee]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1Betay.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:2ac873ee]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:2ac873ee]
end
hold off; %[output:2ac873ee]
axis equal; %[output:2ac873ee]
xlabel("x") %[output:2ac873ee]
ylabel("y") %[output:2ac873ee]
zlabel("$\beta_y$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:2ac873ee]
legend("FEM", "", "analytical", Location="best") %[output:2ac873ee]
title("Distribution of rotation field $\beta_y$ using Q1 elements", ... %[output:group:2d71e9e4] %[output:2ac873ee]
    Interpreter="latex") %[output:group:2d71e9e4] %[output:2ac873ee]
%[text] ![](text:image:01d7) **Try**
%[text] Plot the current configuration of the plate using bilinear quadrilateral elements and reduced-integration
