%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0307A884] ## Transverse-shear locking due incompatible discretization spaces
%[text] The transverse-shear locking can be attributed to the incompatibility of the discretization space for the vertical displacement and rotation fields. This manifests as residual transverse-shear forces for thin plates which trigger this numerical artifact. More information on this topic you can find [here](file:.\main_Chapter41_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_ShearForces.m) based on the Timoshenko beam problem.
%[text] In this section the shear forces $q\_x = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{\\left( \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y \\right)}^{\\gamma\_x}$are computed using both the bilinear and the biquadratic elements along the line $X = 0.5$ m.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EFD4B203] ### Number of evaluation points
numPts = 102; %[control:slider:50d6]{"position":[10,13]}
%[text] ![](text:image:1e27) **Try**
%[text] Modify the number of evaluation points by means of variable `numPts`
%[text] ![](text:image:30a6) **Reflect**
%[text] - How do the different distributions of the transverse-shear force change with respect to the number of evaluation points and why?
%[text] - How many evaluation points do you need to appropriately describe the different transverse-shear force distributions? Is this value constant or does it depend on the chosen value for the beam's length and thickness, $L$ and $t$, respectively? \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9FB0079C] ### Transverse-shear force distribution along the the plate's section
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5A961381] Navigate to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter41_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_ShearForces.m) Section for the detailed analysis.
main_Chapter41_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_ShearForces %[text:anchor:H_47EB6880] %[output:3aac9894]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_190244CA] ## Slenderness study on transverse shear-locking
%[text] In this section it is investigated the behavior of the convergence as the slenderness (length over thickness) goes to infinity $L/t \\rightarrow \\infty$, namely, as the thickness goes to zero $t \\rightarrow 0$. As the thickness goes to zero, the spurious forces due to transverse shear locking increase, leading to an ever increasing artificially stiffening behavior. To keep the solution constant the same scaling factor is applied both to the applied force and the thickness, so that the ratio $p/t$, and thus the resulting deformation, remains constant.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_49EF30BC] ### Bounding exponents of ten
%[text] The space of the slenderness ratio is defined by means of power of ten, namely, $\\lambda \\in \\left\[ \\begin{array}{cc} 10^{\\text{\\texttt{mPowL}}}, 10^{\\text{\\texttt{mPowU}}} \\end{array} \\right\]$
mPowL = -3; %[control:slider:5f2d]{"position":[9,11]}
mPowU = 3; %[control:slider:30d2]{"position":[9,10]}
if floor(mPowL) ~= mPowL || floor(mPowU) ~= mPowU
    error("Variables 'mPowL' and 'mPowU' must be integers")
end
if mPowL >= mPowU
    error("Integer 'mPowL' must be strictly smaller than integer 'mPowU'")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:8594) **Try**
%[text] Modify the bounding values of the slenderness $\\lambda $ by means of variables `mPowL` and `mPowU`
%[text] ![](text:image:96ec) **Reflect**
%[text] How does the slenderness space affect the slenderness graph below and why?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7FB37A19] ### Slenderness study
%[text] Navigate to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter42_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_SlendernessStudy.m) Section for the detailed analysis.
main_Chapter42_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_SlendernessStudy %[output:072aa11e]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A95BCFDE] ## Convergence study on transverse shear-locking
%[text] Another way to identify transverse shear locking is to perform a convergence study for different slenderness levels of the structure. A reduced convergence rate as the slenderness level increases indicates the presence of transverse shear-locking. In extreme transverse shear-locking cases the convergence rate drops to nearly zero
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E8397491] ### Number of refinement levels
numRef = 50; %[control:slider:2e8b]{"position":[10,12]}
if floor(numRef) ~= numRef
    error("Variables 'numRef' must be an integer")
end
if numRef <= 0
    error("Variable 'numRef' must be strictly positive")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:241e) **Try**
%[text] Modify the number of refinment levels using variable `numRef` for different slenderness values $\\lambda$
%[text] ![](text:image:681f) **Reflect**
%[text] How is the convergence rate affected by the different slenderness values $\\lambda$?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0801B508] ### Choose point onto which to compute the displacement
xyPostProc = [XLx (0 + YLy)/2];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E4F5902C] ### Convergence study based on the tip plate's displacement
%[text] Navigate to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter43_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_ConvergenceStudy.m) section for the detailed analysis.
main_Chapter43_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate_ConvergenceStudy %[output:72fe9bcb]
